(i) Consider the non-negative adapted process \(\{X_n, \mathscr{F}_n\}_{n \geq 1}\) and define the partial sum of conditional expectations as \(\Lambda_T := \sum_{n=1}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathscr{F}_{n-1}]\). First, we compute the expectation of \(\Lambda_T\) \[ \mathbb{E}[\Lambda_T] = \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathscr{F}_{n-1}]ight] = \sum_{n=1}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n] \leq M. \] Since \(X_n\) are non-negative, we know that \(\Lambda_T\) is a non-decreasing sequence. Because \(\mathbb{E}(\Lambda_T)\ ( orall\ T\ge 1)\) is also bounded by $M$, we apply the \emph{Lebesgue's Monotone Convergence} theorem. Thus, \(\Lambda_T\) converges almost surely to a limit \(\Lambda\): \[ \Lambda := \lim_{T 	o \infty} \Lambda_T = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathscr{F}_{n-1}] \quad 	ext{a.s.} \] This concludes that the sequence of conditional expectation sums converges almost surely. (ii) We begin by normalizing \(X_{n}\) by considering the expression \(Y_{n}=  rac{X_{n}}{2M}\). According to the \emph{Lebesgue's Monotone Convergence} theorem, we only need to prove that $$ orall\ p \ge 1,\ \ \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]ight]^{p}:=M(p)<+\infty.$$ Next, we proceed with the calculation, and we obtain that $ orall\ p\ge 2,$ there is  egin{align} M(p)&=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]ight]^{p}= \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}Y_{n}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p}
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(a)} \mathbb{E}\left[ rac{1}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p} \mathop{\le}^{(b)}2^{p-1}\left( rac{1}{2^p}+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p}ight)
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(c)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-1}C_{p}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n}|^{2}ight]^{p/2}\mathop{\le}^{(d)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-1}C_{p}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n}|ight]^{p/2}
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(f)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-2}C_{p}+2^{ rac{3}{2}p-2}C_{p}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]ight]^{p/2}
otag\&= rac{1}{2}+2^{p-2}C_{p}+2^{ rac{3}{2}p-2}C_{p}M(p/2). \end{align} In the above derivation, Inequality $(a)$ requires noting that $\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}Y_{n}= rac{1}{2}.$ Inequality $(b)$ uses the \emph{AM-GM} inequality, specifically, \[  igg( rac{a+b}{2} igg)^{p} \leq  rac{a^{p} + b^{p}}{2}. \] Inequality $(c)$ involves using \emph{Burkholder's} inequality, ootnote{	extbf{\emph{Burkholder's} inequality}: For any martingale \((M_n, \mathscr{F}_n)\) with \(M_0 = 0\) almost surely, and for any \(1 \leq p < \infty\), there exist constants \(c_p > 0\) and \(C_p > 0\) depending only on \(p\) such that: \[c_p \, \mathbb{E}[(S(M))^p] \leq \mathbb{E}[(M^*)^p] \leq C_p \, \mathbb{E}[(S(M))^p],\] where \(M^* = \sup_{n \geq 0} |M_n|\) and \(S(M) = \left( \sum_{i \geq 1} (M_i - M_{i-1})^2 ight)^{1/2}\).} where $C_{p}$ is a constant depending only on $p$, and its order with respect to $p$ is $\mathcal{O}(p)$. Inequality $(d)$ requires noting that $$|\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n}|^{2} \leq |\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n}|.$$ By repeatedly using Equation (ef{iteration}) and using the fact that $C_{p} = \mathcal{O}(p)$, we obtain the following estimate \[ M(p) = o(p^{\sqrt{p}}), \] that is, \[\mathbb{E}[\Lambda^{p}]=o((2M)^{p}\cdot p^{\sqrt{p}}). 	ag*{\qedhere}\]

(i) Consider the non-negative adapted process \(\{X_n, \mathscr{F}_n\}_{n \geq 1}\) and define the partial sum of conditional expectations as \(\Lambda_{k,T} := \sum_{n=k}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathscr{F}_{n-k}]\). First, we compute the expectation of \(\Lambda_{k,T}\) \[ \mathbb{E}[\Lambda_{k,T}] = \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathscr{F}_{n-k}]ight] = \sum_{n=k}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n]< \sum_{n=1}^{T} \mathbb{E}[X_n]\leq M. \] Since \(X_n\) are non-negative, we know that \(\Lambda_{k,T}\) is a non-decreasing sequence, and considering that \(\mathbb{E}(\Lambda_{k,T})\ ( orall\ T\ge 1)\) is also bounded by $M$, we apply the \emph{Lebesgue's Monotone Convergence} theorem. Thus, \(\Lambda_{k,T}\) converges almost surely to a limit \(\Lambda^{(k)}\) \[ \Lambda^{(k)} := \lim_{T 	o \infty} \Lambda_{k,T} = \sum_{n=k}^{\infty} \mathbb{E}[X_n \mid \mathscr{F}_{n-k}] \quad 	ext{a.s.} \] This concludes that the sequence of conditional expectation sums converges almost surely. (ii) We begin by normalizing \(X_{n}\) by considering the expression \(Y_{n}=  rac{X_{n}}{2M}\). According to the \emph{Lebesgue's Monotone Convergence} theorem, we only need to prove that $$ orall\ p \ge 1,\ \ \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]ight]^{p}:=M_{k}(p)<+\infty.$$ Next, we proceed with the calculation, and we obtain\ $ orall\ p\ge 2,$ there is:  egin{align} M(p)&=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\sum_{n=k,n\ 	ext{mod}\ k=i}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]ight]^{p}= \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k}^{\infty}Y_{n}+\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\sum_{n=k,n\ 	ext{mod}\ k=i}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p}
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(a)} \mathbb{E}\left[ rac{1}{2}+\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\sum_{n=k,n\ 	ext{mod}\ k=i}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p} \mathop{\le}^{(b)}2^{p-1}\left( rac{1}{2^p}+\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p}ight)
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(c)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-1}k^{p-1}C_{p}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k,n\ 	ext{mod}\ k=i}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n})ight]^{p}
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(d)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-1}k^{{p}-1}C_{p}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k,n\ 	ext{mod}\ k=i}^{\infty}(\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n})^{2}ight]^{p/2}
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(e)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-1}k^{{p}-1}C_{p}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k,n\ 	ext{mod}\ k=i}^{\infty}|\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n}|ight]^{p/2}
otag\&\mathop{\le}^{(f)} rac{1}{2}+2^{p-1}k^{{p}-1}C_{p}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=k}^{\infty}|\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n}|ight]^{p/2}
otag\&\le  rac{1}{2}+2^{p-2}k^{p-1}C_{p}+2^{ rac{3}{2}p-2}k^{p-1}C_{p}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-1}]ight]^{p/2}
otag\&= rac{1}{2}+2^{p-2}k^{p-1}C_{p}+2^{ rac{3}{2}p-2}k^{p-1}C_{p}M(p/2). \end{align} In the above derivation, Inequality $(a)$ is by noting that $\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty}Y_{n}= rac{1}{2}.$ Inequality $(b)$ uses the \emph{AM-GM} inequality, specifically, \[  igg( rac{a+b}{2} igg)^{p} \leq  rac{a^{p} + b^{p}}{2}. \] Inequality $(c)$ involves using the \emph{AM-GM} inequality for $k$ variables, specifically, \[\left( rac{a_{1}+a_{2}+...+a_{k}}{k}ight)^{p}\le \left( rac{a_{1}^{p}+a_{2}^{p}+...+a_{k}^{p}}{k}ight).\] Inequality $(e)$ involves using \emph{Burkholder's} inequality, where $C_{p}$ is a constant depending only on $p$, and its order with respect to $p$ is $\mathcal{O}(p)$. Inequality $(d)$ is by noting that $$|\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n}|^{2} \leq |\mathbb{E}[Y_{n}|\mathscr{F}_{n-k}]-Y_{n}|.$$ By repeatedly using Equation (ef{iteration2}) and using the fact that $C_{p} = \mathcal{O}(p)$, we obtain the following estimate \[ M(p) = o((kp)^{\sqrt{p}}), \] that is, \[\mathbb{E}[(\Lambda^{(k)})^{p}]=o((2M)^{p}\cdot (kp)^{\sqrt{p}}).\] (iii) For any \(0 < l < 1\), we obtain:  egin{align*} \Lambda(l)&=\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\sum_{t=k}^{+\infty}l^{t-k}X_{t}ight) igg|\mathscr{F}_{k-1}ight]=\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}X_{k+t}ight) igg|\mathscr{F}_{k-1}ight]\&=\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[l^{t}X_{k+t} igg|\mathscr{F}_{k-1}ight]=\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}\Lambda^{(t)}. \end{align*} Next, we apply Hölder's inequality, we obtain $ orall\ n\ge 2$  egin{align*} \Lambda(l)^{n}&=\left(\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}\Lambda^{(t)}ight)^{n}\le \left( rac{1}{1-l}ight)^{n-1}\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}(\Lambda^{(t)})^{n}. \end{align*} (iv) Then we have:  egin{align*} \mathbb{E}[e^{p\Lambda(l)}] & = \sum_{n=0}^{+\infty}  rac{p^{n}\mathbb{E}[\Lambda(l)^n]}{n!}\le\sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \left( rac{p}{1-l}ight)^{n} rac{\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}(\Lambda^{(t)})^{n}}{n!} =\sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}(\Lambda^{(t)})^{n}\left( rac{p}{1-l}ight)^{n} rac{1}{n!} \&\mathop{=}^{(iii)}\mathcal{O}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}(2M)^{n}\cdot (tp)^{\sqrt{n}}\left( rac{p}{1-l}ight)^{n} rac{1}{n!}ight)\&=\mathcal{O}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \left(\sum_{t=0}^{+\infty}l^{t}t^{\sqrt{n}}ight)\left( rac{p}{1-l}ight)^{n} rac{(2M)^{n}\cdot (p)^{\sqrt{n}}}{n!}ight)\&\mathop{=}^{	ext{Lemma ef{esti}}}\mathcal{O}\left(\sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \Gamma(\sqrt{n}+1) rac{1}{\left( \ln  rac{1}{l} ight)^{\sqrt{n} + 1}}\left( rac{p}{1-l}ight)^{n} rac{(2M)^{n}\cdot (p)^{\sqrt{n}}}{n!}ight). \end{align*} By substituting the factorial in the denominator with Stirling's approximation, it is evident that the series inside the $\mathcal{O}$ notation converges and depends only on $p,\ l$ and $M$. The lemma follows.

